        "Firstly, numerous scholarly schools of thought support the idea that cognitive processes do not require biological components but instead require other conditions which artificial intelligence can achieve. These mental processes take in information from the environment and allow for problem-solving - a defined imperative for intelligence. One such supporting school is Machine Functionalism, introduced by Hilary Putnam and Jerry Fodor, which posits that cognitive processes can be understood as rule-governed symbol manipulation (Rescorla, 2015). Furthermore, the mental states that constitute cognition can be viewed as manipulating symbols according to formal rules (Rescorla, 2015). This perspective on cognition is described as involving Turing-style computations (Rescorla, 2015). Consequently, machine functionalists believe that cognition is akin to how a computer operates on data. This is independent of any physical human parts and can be fulfilled by technology.",
