        "The philosophical theory of Multiple Realizability also argues that a single mental state or cognitive process can be realized through diverse physical substrates (Bechtel & Mundale, 1999). While this is used as a common critique against the unrelated Brain State theory, it also connects to the subject (Bickle, 1998). Cognitive functions are not bound to a specific biological structure but can be instantiated across different material configurations (Bechtel & Mundale, 1999). Following this logic, computational systems can be included as they suggest that what matters is the function, not its specific physical composition. Thus, even without the biological components found in traditionally \"intelligent\" beings, AI can achieve equivalent cognitive capabilities regardless of whether it uses biological neurons or artificial computational units like modern neural networks.",
