        "A counter argument toward the seriousness of addressing social media addiction is that many other forms of addiction have the same negative outcomes. For instance, cigarettes have very poor health effects as well, such as cancer (Dai et al., 2022). The cigarette industry similarly wants people to stay addicted for profit nonetheless. Meanwhile, the gambling industry also uses operant conditioning to keep players hooked, psychologically invading one's cognition (Delfabbro et al., 2023). Although some of social media addiction’s complications manifest in other addictions, it is particularly deplorable as social media users handcraft a part of their reliance. With more detail, this is because users are providing information that trains the very addictive systems that trap them through previously explained strategies such as A/B testing and adaptive algorithms. Arguably, social media addiction is more contemptible because the person is unknowingly being used against themselves. This goes beyond the moral disrespect of it all – drugs typically change the addict by increasing their craving, but social media, additionally changes itself to make it more attractive to the user as the particular person uses it more (Bhargava & Velasquez, 2021). This makes it doubly frightening, emphasizing social media addiction's unique and dire need to be addressed."
