        "Decisively, social media platforms are profit-driven systems intentionally engineered to challenge cognitive autonomy, creating reliance. Users are hooked in by reinforcing mechanisms that can be explained through an operant learning style, simultaneously trapped in self-sustaining cycles of engagement that are similar to dynamical systems. People also increasingly offload mental processes onto these platforms, lessening their cognitive power. The outcomes of social media are also drastic, ranging from mental to social to physical. While parallels can be drawn to other dependencies, social media is uniquely insidious. This is because social media adapts in response to the very data users inadvertently provide, presenting an immoral and disrespectful business model. One author aptly noted this unnerving and unprecedented reality – a couple of engineers and a handful of companies have power over 2 billion humans' decisions and thoughts (Bhargava & Velasquez, 2021). Unfortunately, these people do not have the public’s best interests at heart. People must demand transparency, invest in digital literacy, and enforce government regulations prioritizing user well-being. This trajectory will not be disrupted if left in the hands of these money-oriented business leaders. Rather, if action is not taken, even more users will be drawn into cognitive subordination, advertised as essential and enjoyable tools. In such a universe, these corporations will continue to amass financial power and influence, while society remains placid in its own exploitation."
